{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And of pure myrrh five hundred</b>. The commentators and the Rav, Rabbi Moshe (Maimonides, Laws of Temple Vessels 1:2), among them all agreed that the mor is what is called musk. But R. Abraham ibn Ezra objected that it is not a spice, even though its fragrance is good. And perhaps for this reason Scripture separated it from the spices. And he raised a difficulty, for it is written (Song of Songs 5:1), \"I gathered my myrrh\" — which implies something collected, and those who bring it say that it is produced in the gland of the gazelle. And furthermore, from the verse (Song of Songs 5:5), \"And my hands dripped with myrrh.\" Perhaps this is so when it is moist. And it is possible that he says \"I gathered my myrrh\" because it is blood gathered in the belly of an animal resembling the gazelle known in the land of India, and when it walks among the bushes in the days of great heat it scratches against that pouch, and the blood comes out clotted, and they gather it from the meadow. And he said, \"And my hands dripped with myrrh,\" because Scripture likens its fragrance as though his hands would drip with that fragrance like drops of water.\n\nOthers (Raavad, Laws of Temple Vessels 1:3) said: how could the blood of an unclean animal enter into the incense and the anointing oil? This too is no difficulty, for that moisture which is collected in it from the abundance of blood and which oozes from it during its life contains neither impurity nor repulsiveness. And they explained (Ibn Ezra in the short commentary, and Radak in the Book of Roots, root דרר) that deror is from the expression \"and you shall proclaim deror\" (Leviticus 25:10), meaning that it should be free of admixture and foreign matter. Or perhaps Scripture required it to be free to say that it should be taken from that gazelle while it is free, walking among the beds of spices and enjoying itself as it wishes, for when it is captured and kept under human control it does not produce myrrh except a little, and it is not fragrant. And this is clear.\n\nYet despite all this, it appears to me from the words of our Sages that the mor is not musk. For they said in Midrash Chazita (Song of Songs Rabbah 4:14): Mor — this is inmar'non. And musk, even in the language of the Sages, its name is thus: musk, as they said in Berakhot (43a): \"with the exception of musk, because it is a species of animal.\" And so too there in the Jerusalem Talmud (Chapter 6, Halakhah 6): \"with the exception of musk.\" And the author of the Arukh (entry מסק) wrote that its name is likewise thus in Greek. And he further said in Midrash Chazita (Song of Songs Rabbah 1:58): \"A bundle of myrrh is my beloved to me\" — this is Abraham. Just as this mor is the chief of all kinds of spices, so Abraham is the chief of all the righteous. Just as this mor gives off fragrance only in fire, so Abraham's deeds were not known until he was cast into the fiery furnace. And just as this mor — all who gather it, their hands become bitter, so Abraham embittered himself and afflicted himself with sufferings. But musk gives off its fragrance without fire! And furthermore, we learned (Mikva'ot 9:5): \"These interpose in vessels: pitch and mor,\" etc., on the mallet. Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel says: up to an Italian issar. And they taught there (Mishnah 7): This is the principle — anything one is particular about interposes, and anything one is not particular about does not interpose. But musk is not something that adheres like pitch so that it would interpose, and even if they prepare it in a manner that it adheres to vessels, one is not particular about it interposing even on the mallet. And the Targum of \"Myrrh and aloes, cassia — all your garments\" (Psalms 45:9) is: mora and eksilyan and ketzi'ata.\n\nWhat is likely is that it is what is called by this name in Arabic: mor, which has several varieties — mor ahmar and mor abyad — and with it they burn incense, and its fragrance spreads through fire even more. And all the tongues — Hebrew and Aramaic, and also the Arabic ones — are equal regarding it. And in the language of the Aggadah, Persian or Greek, it is called anmarnon, which is close to this, and also in the tongue of the Romans it is called mira. And from the correspondence of the languages regarding it, it appears that it is that species, and it is reckoned among the aromatics. And what they said there (Song of Songs Rabbah, same place) — that it is the chief of all spices — is because Scripture mentioned it at their head, or because in burning it is the most praised of all. And it is possible that among its varieties there is found one with an even more fragrant aroma, and that is what is called deror, and the one who gathers this — his hands become bitter, for it is as bitter as wormwood. And they taught in Sifra (Leviticus, Houva, Chapter 12): Things that are impossible to detect, such as mixing water into wine, komos into mor — for they adulterate it with komos, which is called the gum of the Arab tree, which resembles it. Therefore it says \"pure myrrh [mor deror],\" that it should be clean from the adulteration with which they constantly adulterate it.\n\nAnd it is possible that the expression deror everywhere means cleanliness/purity, as in \"and you shall proclaim deror in the land to all its inhabitants\" — that all who possess the land should be clean from servitude and from any bondage upon their bodies and their lands, like the expression \"and the owner of the ox is clean [naki]\" (Exodus 21:28).\n\nAnd what Scripture says, \"And my hands dripped with myrrh, and my fingers with flowing myrrh\" (Song of Songs 5:5) — it is possible that the reading is: \"And my hands dripped with the oil of mor,\" for the custom is to anoint the hands with it as well, to adorn and soften them, as it is written (Esther 2:12), \"six months with oil of mor,\" and they explained there (Megillah 13a): olive oil that did not lack a third, because it makes the hair fall out and softens the flesh. And the matter is that this oil is prepared with mor, and is called by that name, and this is the meaning of \"dripped.\" And I believe that it is the inmarnon mentioned in the Midrash (Song of Songs Rabbah 4:14), for thus they call it in the Jerusalem Talmud (Demai 1:3): \"rose oil\" — vardino, and so is the way of names for oils in the tongues of the nations. And it is also possible that they extract oil from the mor, just as is done with mastich and other gums, and it would be proper to call it mor and the oil of mor." "<b>And of cinnamon, sweet-smelling, half of it</b>. Since cinnamon is the bark of a tree, there are some that are good and have fragrance and taste, and some that are nothing but wood. Therefore it was necessary to say: cinnamon of the good kind. This is the language of Rashi (on Exodus 30:23). And the Rav, Rabbi Moshe (in his Commentary on the Mishnah, Keritot 1:1), said that it is \"qishr salikha.\" And others said (Ibn Ezra, short commentary) that it is the good wood called \"utayyib.\" But this is not like the words of any of them, for they said in Bereshit Rabbah (65:17) and in Midrash Chazita (Song of Songs Rabbah, same place) that cinnamon used to grow in the land of Israel, and goats and gazelles would eat of it — and if so, it is like the grass of the field, which flocks graze.\n\nIn my opinion it is the aromatic straw called in Arabic adbar, and in the tongue of the Romans ashqinant, and it is a valued spice, and in la'az it is \"saiyka demika,\" and it is a pasture for camels in its place. And our master the author of the Halakhot wrote in Pesachim (on the pages of the Rif, 25a): such as cinnamon and sanbal, which resemble straw. And the ketzi'ah is known from the Aramaic tongue, for it is ketzi'ata, and so too in Arabic."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The midrashic transliterated terms (אינמר\"נון / אנמרנון / אינמרנון) appear in different forms across the passage; I have rendered each occurrence exactly as Ramban spells it. Similarly, the Arabic and Romance vernacular terms (אדבר, אשקיננט, סאייק דמיקא, עוטייב, קשר סליכה) are transliterated as Ramban gives them without resolution.", 1 "The vernacular spice terms (קשר סליכה, עוטייב, אדבר, אשקיננט, סאייק דמיקא, סנבל) are transliterated as Ramban gives them without attempting modern botanical identification."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:30.170865657Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}